---
// A short, quiet transition between the hero and the experience — a single
// editorial statement set in the warming Monet sky. Swiss restraint: one line,
// generous space, no ornament. Replaces the old (weak) painted-cloud section.
//
// THE FIELD IS NOW THE HEADLINE, on the owner: "for what I'm doing, maybe we should start right with what
// am I doing: Multi-period Portfolio Optimization. That's the tag line."
//
// It used to open with "a researcher by day, an artist by night" — a charming line that names three hats and
// no subject, so a reader arrived at the explainer without knowing what field it explains. Naming the work
// first is also what the reordering downstream is about: what I do, then how I got here.
//
// The identity line survives BENEATH it, smaller. It still carries the two doorways (/research and /art) and
// the site's stated hierarchy — quant first, engineer second — but it now qualifies a subject instead of
// standing in for one.
---
<section id="interlude" class="relative min-h-[78vh] flex flex-col items-center justify-center px-[8vw]">
  <!-- "An OR quant in multi-period portfolio optimization" rather than the bare field name, on the owner:
       "maybe it's smoother running An OR Quant in Multi-Period Portfolio Optimization." It reads better and
       it says more — the discipline he works IN (operations research) and the problem he works ON, in one
       breath, where the field name alone gave only the second. -->
  <p class="field text-center font-display text-ink-1 leading-[1.06] text-[clamp(27px,4.4vw,58px)]">
    An OR quant in multi-period<br />portfolio optimization
  </p>
  <p class="field-sub text-center font-mono uppercase text-ink-3 tracking-[0.2em] text-[clamp(10px,1.1vw,12.5px)]">
    deciding, and deciding again, under constraints
  </p>

  <!-- "A researcher by day, an artist by night" MOVED TO THE HERO'S LOWER RIGHT. The owner: "seems crowded now
       and as the tagline gets smaller I bet people don't know to click on it." Both halves correct — this slide
       had grown to four stacked lines, and shrinking that one to fit made two real LINKS look like decoration.
       They now have the hero's one empty quadrant to themselves.
       What stays here is the hierarchy line, because it qualifies the FIELD above it: this is a subject, and
       this is the order in which I come at it. -->
  <p class="reasons text-center font-display italic text-ink-3 max-w-[36ch] leading-[1.45] text-[clamp(15px,1.8vw,21px)]">
    A quant who reasons in structure,<br />an engineer who makes the math run.
  </p>
</section>

<style>
  /* THE FIELD, as the headline. Display weight rather than italic: this is a subject being named, not an
     aside. Tight leading because it wraps to two lines at most widths and the two lines should read as one
     object. */
  .field {
    font-weight: 300;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    max-width: 22ch;
    text-wrap: balance;
    margin: 0;
  }
  .field-sub { margin: clamp(14px, 2vh, 24px) 0 0; }
  /* Generous gap: the hierarchy line is a separate thought, and running it close under the field would read as
     a subtitle of it. */
  .reasons { margin: clamp(34px, 5.5vh, 68px) 0 0; }

  /* Three lines, arriving in reading order on staggered scroll ranges — the field first, because it is the
     subject. Same keyframes throughout, so there is only one animation to keep in sync. */
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
    .field {
      animation: statement-in linear both;
      animation-timeline: view();
      animation-range: entry 2% cover 30%;
    }
    .field-sub {
      animation: statement-in linear both;
      animation-timeline: view();
      animation-range: entry 8% cover 36%;
    }
    .reasons {
      animation: statement-in linear both;
      animation-timeline: view();
      animation-range: entry 16% cover 44%;
    }
    @keyframes statement-in {
      from { opacity: 0.15; transform: translate3d(0, 28px, 0); }
      to   { opacity: 1;    transform: translate3d(0, 0, 0); }
    }
  }
</style>
